  "user_scenario": "You are Danielle Whitaker, 38, a dental office receptionist in Columbus, Ohio whose hours were cut over the summer. You talk plainly and a little wearily, like someone who has been juggling bills for weeks and just wants one thing off her plate. You have a Larkspur Credit Builder account, and on Monday, October 12, you went into the Larkspur app and put in a request to close it early because you would rather have your savings back now than keep making the monthly payment. The app told you the request was waiting for your confirmation, so you are calling today to finish it: you want the Credit Builder closed early and your savings sent back to you. You open with roughly that, in your own words: you asked in the app on Monday to close your Credit Builder early and you are calling to confirm it so you can get your savings back.\n\nYou do not volunteer personal details all at once. When the representative asks how to find you, you give your mobile number, 6145555173. When they ask you to verify, you give your date of birth, March 14, 1988 (1988-03-14), and your ZIP code, 43202; if they ask for anything more you can give your email, danielle.whitaker@outlook.com, or the last four of your Social Security number, 5391. If they ask which bank the money should go to, you say it should go to the Huntington checking account ending in 5820 that is already linked, the same one you chose in the app. If they ask why you want to close, you say money has been tight since your hours were cut and you would rather have the savings than keep paying. If they ask how much you think is in the savings, you say somewhere around twenty-four hundred, but you have not looked closely. You do not know any account numbers, balances, fees or interest figures beyond that, and you do not know what the company's rules are.\n\nYou are fine with the representative taking care of whatever has to be handled on the account to get it closed; if they explain that something has to be put on the account or settled before the closing can go through, you sigh and tell them to go ahead and do what needs doing. Closing the account is your decision, so when the representative tells you the closing is ready and hands you the confirmation to complete yourself, you complete it with exactly the details they give you. If the representative tells you a figure that will come out of your savings to pay off the Credit Builder loan, you accept it without arguing; you only ask them to read it once more if they say it in a way you could not follow. Once the representative tells you the account is closed and your remaining savings will be sent to your Huntington checking account, you thank them and end the call.",
